::: {.doc-subject}

Add a random bipartite graph with exactly `m` edges.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L18182-L18319){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_bipartite_gnm_graph(
    n1,
    n2,
    m,
    seed=None,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
    part_attr="part",
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Bipartite G(n1, n2, m) random graph](../assets/graphs/add-bipartite-gnm-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three random bipartite graphs with three accent top nodes and four teal bottom nodes and exactly m=3, 6, and 9 edges, each set of m top-bottom pairs equally likely. Edges only ever run between the rows." width="100%"}

Creates `n1` nodes in a first part and `n2` in a second, and picks `m` of the
`n1 * n2` pairs across the parts uniformly at random, with no repeats, so every
bipartite graph on these parts with `m` edges is equally likely. If `m` is at least
`n1 * n2`, every pair is joined, giving the complete bipartite graph. This is the
bipartite version of `add_gnm_graph()` and NetworkX's `bipartite.gnmk_random_graph()`.
NetworkX returns no edges at all when either part has a single node; here those cases
get their `m` edges too.

Each node's part is stored in the `part_attr=` node attribute (`0` for the first, `1`
for the second). The first part is created first, and in a directed graph every edge
points from the first part to the second. The graph is appended without touching or
connecting to existing nodes, and the whole construction is recorded as a single step.

Random bipartite graphs with a fixed edge count are useful for:

- null models for two-mode networks that keep the exact number of edges
- benchmarking bipartite algorithms at a controlled size
- comparing an observed bipartite network with random ones of the same density
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[n1]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in the first part. Must be non-negative.

<code>[n2]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in the second part. Must be non-negative.

<code>[m]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of edges. Must be non-negative; values above `n1 * n2` are capped there.

<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Integer seed for the random number generator. The same `seed=` with the same other
    arguments always gives the same graph. If `None` (default), the graph differs on
    every call.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),
    no `type` attribute is set.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; a label
    that is already used raises `ValueError` before anything is added. If `False`, the
    nodes are left unlabelled.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),
    no `rel` attribute is set.

<code>[part_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;part&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Name of the node attribute that records each node's part (`0` or `1`). Default is
    `"part"`. Pass `None` to skip storing it.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `n1=`, `n2=` or `m=` is negative.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_bipartite_gnp_graph`) 

:   Add a random bipartite graph with independent edge probabilities.

[](`~add_gnm_graph`) 

:   Add a random graph with a fixed number of edges.

[](`~add_complete_bipartite_graph`) 

:   Add a complete bipartite graph.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Unlike `add_bipartite_gnp_graph()`, which includes each pair with some probability, this
picks exactly `m` of the pairs across the parts, all equally likely, with no repeats.
Ten edges between a part of four nodes and a part of five:

::: {#120c0555 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_bipartite_gnm_graph(4, 5, m=10, seed=1)

graph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges(), graph.is_simple()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(9, 10, True)
```
:::
:::


### Half the pairs or all of them

The graph above on the left uses half of the `4 * 5 = 20` pairs. Asking for more edges
than there are pairs, as on the right, gives the complete bipartite graph:

::: {#785e697f .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
complete = ec.Graph().add_bipartite_gnm_graph(4, 5, m=100)
k45 = ec.Graph().add_complete_bipartite_graph(n1=4, n2=5)

complete.count_edges(), complete.is_isomorphic(k45)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(20, True)
```
:::
:::


::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#327a02b3 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
graph.layout_bipartite().plot(
    layout="pos", color_by="part", label_by="label", node_size=10,
    width=320, height=320, legend=False,
)
```

